  "description": "Here is how to read the Kimi K3 config.json. The architecture class is KimiLinearForCausalLM, which is the transformers (or equivalent) module that wires embeddings, attention, the MLP, and the language-modelling head that turns hidden states into next-token logits.\n\nhidden_size is 7,168: that is the width of every residual stream vector. The embedding matrix is therefore 163,840 \u00d7 7,168: each token id owns a vector of that width.\n\nnum_attention_heads is 96, so each head sees 128 dimensions (7,168 / 96). The ratio test is hidden_size / num_attention_heads = 7168 / 96 = 128. 128 is a multiple of 32 (NVIDIA warp-friendly), and a multiple of 16 (Huawei cube-friendly), and a power of two (Triton / FlashAttention-friendly). From this number alone you cannot name the training chip.\n\nnum_key_value_heads is 96, so this is multi-head attention (MHA): every query head keeps its own key/value.\n\nnum_hidden_layers is 93: the stack is that many transformer blocks deep.\n\nintermediate_size is 33,792. In a SwiGLU MLP this is the high-dimensional workspace: the 7,168-wide stream is projected up, activated with situ, then projected back. Labs usually round this width to a multiple of 128 or 256 so it tiles on the chip.\n\nmax_position_embeddings is 1,048,576 tokens \u2014 the context window in the config. There is no rope_scaling entry, so the rotary positions are used as-is.\n\nvocab_size is 163,840. 163,840 is divisible by 256, the usual vocab-tiling grain. bos/eos ids, when present, are just two special rows in that table; the padded vocab is often larger than the tokenizer\u2019s real inventory.\n\nThis is a mixture of experts: 896 routed experts with top-k 16. The 1991 Jacobs / Jordan / Nowlan / Hinton paper \u201cAdaptive Mixtures of Local Experts\u201d is the ancestor of this router-plus-specialists idea.\n\ntie_word_embeddings is false, so the input embedding matrix and the output LM head are separate learned matrices rather than a shared transpose.\n\ntorch_dtype is bfloat16. Brain floating point keeps FP32\u2019s exponent range with less mantissa, which avoids the overflow/underflow of a tiny exponent at the cost of noisier decimals \u2014 and it halves the on-chip footprint versus FP32. NVIDIA A100s were the first NVIDIA chips with native BF16; Huawei Ascend 910-class parts also prefer BF16/FP16 on the cube.\n\nThe copy of config.json is from https://huggingface.co/moonshotai/Kimi-K3/blob/main/config.json."
